var records=[{"names":["copper citrate"],"role":"Copper form","form":"Copper citrate names a citrate-associated copper form. The label does not independently verify precise composition, hydration state, manufacturing origin, elemental copper amount, or absorbed dose. It is not automatically natural, food-derived, or superior.","use":"Copper source","source":"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Copper-HealthProfessional/","purpose":"A copper source. Copper is an essential trace mineral used by enzymes involved in energy production, iron metabolism, and connective tissue synthesis. These normal functions do not establish an added benefit from this product.","difference":"NIH reports that comparative bioavailability studies of supplemental copper forms are lacking. The name does not establish a best form, absorbed dose, or cardiovascular or cognitive benefit. Excessive copper exposure can cause gastrointestinal symptoms and liver damage; high zinc intakes can reduce copper absorption. These concerns depend on amounts and context, not a label match alone.","formUnspecified":false,"reviewed":"2026-10-11"},{"names":["molybdenum"],"role":"Molybdenum · form not specified","form":"The label names molybdenum without its supplying compound or preparation. Form, manufacturing source, elemental amount, and absorbed dose are unspecified. Sodium molybdate or another specific compound must not be inferred.","use":"Molybdenum source","source":"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Molybdenum-HealthProfessional/","purpose":"A molybdenum source. Molybdenum is part of a cofactor required by enzymes involved in processing sulfur-containing amino acids and other compounds. This normal nutritional role does not establish a detox effect, treatment, or added benefit from this product.","difference":"NIH reports no comparative bioavailability studies among supplemental molybdenum forms. Deficiency is very rare, and molybdenum is not a standard treatment for disease. Excess-intake guidance relies on limited human evidence and animal findings. The label name does not establish absorbed dose, nutritional need, or individual safety.","formUnspecified":true,"reviewed":"2026-10-11"},{"names":["sodium molybdate"],"role":"Molybdenum form","form":"Sodium molybdate is a named molybdenum-containing compound used as a molybdenum source. The label does not establish hydration state, manufacturing origin, elemental molybdenum amount, absorbed dose, or a meaningful sodium contribution.","use":"Molybdenum source","source":"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Molybdenum-HealthProfessional/","purpose":"A molybdenum source. Molybdenum is part of a cofactor required by enzymes involved in processing sulfur-containing amino acids and other compounds. This normal nutritional role does not establish a detox effect, treatment, or added benefit from this product.","difference":"NIH reports no comparative bioavailability studies among supplemental molybdenum forms. Deficiency is very rare, and molybdenum is not a standard treatment for disease. Excess-intake guidance relies on limited human evidence and animal findings. The label name does not establish absorbed dose, nutritional need, or individual safety.","formUnspecified":false,"reviewed":"2026-10-11"},{"names":["pyridoxal-5-phosphate","pyridoxal 5-phosphate","pyridoxal 5 phosphate","pyridoxal-5'-phosphate","pyridoxal 5'-phosphate","pyridoxal 5' phosphate","pyridoxal phosphate","plp"],"role":"Vitamin B6 form · PLP","form":"Pyridoxal-5-phosphate (PLP) is a phosphorylated B6 form, distinct from pyridoxine hydrochloride. The name does not establish manufacturing source, hydration state, vitamin B6 amount, or absorbed dose. Active coenzyme describes its biochemical identity, not a superior-product claim.","use":"Vitamin B6","source":"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminB6-HealthProfessional/","purpose":"A vitamin B6 source. Pyridoxal phosphate is an active coenzyme form involved in many enzyme reactions, particularly amino acid metabolism. This biochemical role does not prove an added benefit from this product.","difference":"NIH reports that absorption does not differ substantially among supplemental B6 forms. Phosphorylated forms are dephosphorylated before absorption, so active coenzyme identity does not establish direct uptake or superiority. Excessive supplemental B6 exposure can cause nerve damage; much of the toxicity evidence concerns pyridoxine. This does not establish a PLP-specific risk at this product's dose or a safety exemption for PLP. Amount, duration, total intake, and medication context are required.","formUnspecified":false,"reviewed":"2026-10-11"}];
