var LIMITS=" The name does not verify amount, purity, hydration or isomer composition, feedstock, manufacturing details, final pH, or allergy cross-contact.",CONTEXT="An acidity or flavor function is not a health benefit. Food-use rules apply to specified ingredients, categories, and conditions, not a universal safety verdict or proof of product compliance. A name does not establish total dietary exposure or an individual effect. Existing health-evidence records, colors, review dates, and allergy/diet rules are unchanged.";
var data=[[["citric acid"],"Acidity control / formulation aid","Citric acid is a named acid, not citrate salts or vitamin C. Occurrence in fruit does not establish that this ingredient was extracted from fruit.","May adjust acidity or flavor and serve other formulation functions. Its exact role and the product's pH are not established by the name.","184.1033",false,""],[["lactic acid"],"Acidity control / flavoring","Lactic acid is a named acid. The word does not identify milk, lactose, fermentation feedstock, isomer, or manufacturing route.","May adjust pH or flavor and serve antimicrobial, curing/pickling, or other specified functions. No dairy-free or allergy-safe conclusion is inferred.","184.1061",false,""],[["malic acid"],"Acidity control / flavoring","Generic malic acid does not identify L versus DL form. Its occurrence in fruit does not establish an apple-derived or otherwise natural manufacturing source.","May provide tartness, enhance flavor, or adjust acidity in specified food uses. The label does not establish a nutritional benefit.","184.1069",false,""],[["fumaric acid"],"Acidity / formulation agent","Fumaric acid is distinct from its sodium, calcium, magnesium, potassium, or ferrous salts. Ferrous fumarate's iron-source use must not be assigned to the acid.","May serve an acid or formulation function in specified foods. Intended effect and amount are not verified by this wording.","172.350",false,""],[["acetic acid","ethanoic acid"],"Acidity control / flavoring","Acetic acid is a named acid, not automatically a vinegar preparation. Fermentation versus synthesis is not established by the name.","May contribute acidity, flavor, pickling/curing effects, or serve other specified functions. It is not evidence of a vinegar-related health benefit.","184.1005",false,""],[["tartaric acid"],"Acidity control / formulation aid","US food-use rules describe food-grade tartaric acid in the L configuration. The name does not independently verify conformance or source; it is not potassium bitartrate/cream of tartar.","May adjust acidity or flavor and serve firming, moisture-retention, or other specified functions. No source or nutritional benefit is inferred.","184.1099",false,""],[["adipic acid"],"Acidity / formulation agent","Adipic acid is a named dicarboxylic acid, not an adipate salt. The label does not independently verify the manufacturing process.","May serve a flavor, acidity, or other specified formulation function. A chemical name alone is not a health or safety finding.","184.1009",false,""],[["phosphoric acid"],"Acidity / formulation agent","Phosphoric acid is a named acid, not interchangeable with the various phosphate salts. Its presence does not establish meaningful phosphorus supplementation.","May contribute acidity and formulation effects. Final pH, total acid exposure, and the actual purpose are not established by the name.","182.1073",false,""],[["potassium citrate"],"Acidity control / citrate salt","A potassium citrate salt is named. The exact preparation and hydration state are not independently verified; it is distinct from citric acid and sodium citrate.","May buffer or control acidity and serve other specified functions. The name does not establish a meaningful potassium contribution or electrolyte benefit.","184.1625",false,""],[["sodium lactate"],"Acidity / moisture control","Sodium lactate is the sodium salt of lactic acid, distinct from sodium stearoyl lactylate. The name does not establish meaningful sodium intake or a dairy source.","May control pH or moisture, affect flavor, assist emulsification, or serve other specified functions. Food-category restrictions and exact use still require checking.","184.1768",false,""],[["monosodium glutamate","msg","monosodium l-glutamate"],"Savory flavor enhancer","MSG is a defined sodium salt of glutamic acid. It is not the same ingredient preparation as yeast extract or a protein hydrolysate.","May enhance savory taste. The label does not establish amount, total glutamate exposure, source feedstock, or an individual response.","https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg",false,"FDA says added MSG must be declared as monosodium glutamate, not hidden under spices or flavoring. Naturally glutamate-containing ingredients remain distinct preparations. This is labeling context, not a product-compliance verdict."],[["disodium inosinate"],"Savory flavoring adjuvant","Disodium inosinate is a named salt of inosinic acid, distinct from disodium guanylate and MSG. Manufacture and dietary source are not inferred.","May enhance savory flavor in formulations. The name does not establish the actual dose, a nutritional effect, or vegetarian/vegan suitability.","172.535",false,""],[["disodium guanylate"],"Savory flavor enhancer","Disodium guanylate is a named guanylic-acid salt, distinct from disodium inosinate and MSG. Its source is not verified from the chemical name.","May enhance savory flavor. Mixtures, source feedstock, and dietary suitability are not established by this term.","172.530",false,""],[["yeast extract","bakers yeast extract","baker's yeast extract"],"Yeast-derived flavor preparation","A yeast extract is a mixture of soluble yeast-cell components. Generic wording does not verify mechanical rupture, autolysis, strain, or the full preparation; it is not pure MSG.","May contribute savory flavor. It can contain free glutamate, but the amount and this product's actual purpose are not established by the name.","184.1983",true,"The US bakers-yeast-extract rule describes a specific mechanically ruptured preparation; generic yeast extract wording is not proof of compliance with that specification."],[["autolyzed yeast","autolysed yeast"],"Yeast-derived flavor preparation","Autolyzed yeast describes yeast broken down using its own enzymes. It is not silently equated with an isolated yeast extract, nutritional yeast, or pure MSG.","May contribute savory flavor and free glutamate. The name does not establish amounts, processing details, or vitamin enrichment.","https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg",true,""],[["autolyzed yeast extract","autolysed yeast extract"],"Yeast-derived extract preparation","This wording specifically names an autolyzed yeast extract. It remains a mixture, not pure MSG; the full composition and process are not independently verified.","May contribute savory flavor and free glutamate. It does not establish a health benefit, vitamin content, or an individual response.","102.22",true,""],[["hydrolyzed vegetable protein","hydrolysed vegetable protein","hvp"],"Protein hydrolysate · plant source unspecified","The wording describes a plant-protein hydrolysate without identifying a specific food source. Soy, wheat, corn, manufacturing route, and residual allergen levels must not be inferred.","May enhance savory flavor and supply free glutamate. It is not pure MSG, and hydrolysis does not establish allergy safety.","102.22",true,"US 21 CFR 102.22 does not accept hydrolyzed vegetable protein as a source-specific common/usual name. This explains a naming limitation; the app does not infer this product's jurisdiction or declare it illegal."],[["hydrolyzed protein","hydrolysed protein"],"Protein hydrolysate · source unspecified","The wording does not identify the original protein or whether it is plant- or animal-derived. It must not be silently treated as hydrolyzed vegetable protein or pure MSG.","May contribute flavor or other formulation effects. Composition, free glutamate, source, and allergy relevance are not established by this generic term.","102.22",true,"US 21 CFR 102.22 requires a source-specific name and does not accept hydrolyzed protein alone. This is regulatory context, not a product-compliance verdict."]];
